The Lighthouse at Marrow Point}
\par \pard\plain \s71\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\qc\keep\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\keepn\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs24\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af12\langfe1033{\loch
Eleanor Vance}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf21\f6\fs32\b\dbch\af8{\loch
{\*\bkmkstart prologue}Prologue}
\par \pard\plain \s67\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
The lamp had not been lit for eleven years when the first letter arrived. It came in a plain brown envelope, addressed in a careful hand to }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
The Keeper}{\loch
, as though the post office had simply forgotten that there was no longer any such person.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
{\*\bkmkstart prologue}Nobody in the village could say who had carried it up the cliff path. By morning it was lying on the step, weighted down with a smooth grey stone.{\*\bkmkend prologue}}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf21\f6\fs32\b\dbch\af8{\loch
{\*\bkmkstart chapter-one}Chapter One}
\par \pard\plain \s67\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
Mara Ellison had inherited three things from her grandfather: a brass telescope, a talent for silence, and the key to a building that the council wanted demolished. She kept all three in the same drawer and tried not to think about any of them.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{
\u8220\'93}{\loch
You could sell it,\u8221\'94 her brother said, not for the first time. \u8220\'93The land alone must be worth something.\u8221\'94}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{
\u8220\'93}{\loch
It is not for sale, Tomas.\u8221\'94}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{
\u8220\'93}{\loch
Everything is for sale. That is the whole point of money.\u8221\'94}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
She did not answer him. Outside the kitchen window the tide was coming in, and the long shadow of the tower lay across the water like the hand of a clock. She had watched that shadow every evening of her childhood and she knew, without looking at her watch, that it was nearly seven.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs24\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af12\langfe1033{
{\field{\*\fldinst SHAPE }{\fldrslt{\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft0\shptop-31\shpright9359\shpbottom-2\shpwr3\shpbxignore\shpbyignore\shpz0{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 3}}{\sp{\sn lineJoinStyle}{\sv 2}}{\sp{\sn lineColor}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn lineBackColor}{\sv 16777215}}{\sp{\sn fshadowObscured}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fillType}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fillColor}{\sv 16777215}}{\sp{\sn fillBackColor}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fNoFillHitTest}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn anchorText}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn wzDescription}{\sv }}{\sp{\sn wzName}{\sv }}}}}}}
\par \pard\plain \s67\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
The letter was waiting when she climbed the path the next morning. She turned it over twice before she opened it, and afterwards she wished that she had left it where it lay.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
Light the lamp on the ninth,}{\loch
 it said. }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
Someone is coming home.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
{\*\bkmkstart chapter-one}There was no signature. There was only a small drawing in the corner of the page, a ship with three masts, and she had seen that ship before, carved into the underside of her grandfather\u8217\'92s desk.{\*\bkmkend chapter-one}}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf21\f6\fs32\b\dbch\af8{\loch
{\*\bkmkstart chapter-two}Chapter Two}
\par \pard\plain \s67\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
The harbour master was a broad, slow-moving woman named Agnes Thorne who had never once been surprised by anything. She read the letter at arm\u8217\'92s length, the way she read everything, and handed it back.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{
\u8220\'93}{\loch
Well,\u8221\'94 she said. \u8220\'93That is your grandfather\u8217\'92s ship, right enough. The }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
Corriveau}{\loch
. Lost with all hands in the winter of sixty-one.\u8221\'94}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{
\u8220\'93}{\loch
I know when it was lost.\u8221\'94}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{
\u8220\'93}{\loch
Then you know nobody is coming home on it.\u8221\'94 Agnes folded her hands. \u8220\'93Somebody is having a joke with you, girl. An unkind one.\u8221\'94}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
Mara wanted to believe her. It would have been the sensible thing to believe. But she had already counted the days, and the ninth was a Thursday, and on Thursday there would be no moon at all.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
{\*\bkmkstart chapter-two}She bought lamp oil on the way back up the hill. She told herself that it was only because the price was good.{\*\bkmkend chapter-two}}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb480\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf21\f6\fs32\b\dbch\af8{\loch
{\*\bkmkstart chapter-three}Chapter Three}
\par \pard\plain \s67\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
By the ninth the whole village knew. They came up the path in ones and twos as the light failed, pretending that they had only come to see the view, and stood about on the wet grass with their collars turned up against the wind.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
Tomas came last, carrying a flask of tea and a blanket, and said nothing at all, which was the kindest thing he had done in years.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar{\loch
At a quarter to nine Mara climbed the one hundred and forty-two steps, trimmed the wick the way she had been taught as a child, and struck a match.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\loch\ql\widctlpar\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\sb180\sa180{\loch
{\*\bkmkstart chapter-three}The beam went out across the black water. For a long moment there was nothing. Then, far out beyond the bar, a second light answered.{\*\bkmkend chapter-three}}
\par }